> While we're off topic: I fear EMACS, but are there any other good programmable
> editors which will run under a (company-enforced) Windows NT?

XEmacs for windows is pretty cozy, in terms of having a nicer GUI (it
was developed under the name "Lucid Emacs" by JWZ, of Netscape fame). 
http://www.xemacs.org

I use Vim (on Windows and Unix).  But if you fear Emacs, Vim may not
be the best choice (modal editing is a love it or hate it thing).  It
can be extended in Perl or Python (or a simple builtin script
language) and has fully programmable syntax highlighting, and it
integrates nicely with Visual Studio (and with standard Unix tools). 
http://www.vim.org

I've heard good things about certain commercial Windows editors, in
particular Zeus, Textpad, Visual Slickedit, and Crisp--they're
commercial, so you'll have to google for them yourself.

But I recommend you learn one of the emacs or vi derivatives, they are
extremely powerful and if you write code on a regular basis it'll pay
off in the long term.  Both XEmacs and Vim have decent tutorials.

